"Wordchains are Artonan chains of words that trade with an Opposite on another world."
"Hand gestures go with the words."
"A wordchain needs sincerity; one not meant does nothing."
"Only Artonan healers and priests can tell whether a chain worked."
"Inflection matters, and shouting does not make a chain stronger."
"A person keeps a balance of times as giver against times as receiver."
"Most hold chains said in English useless."
"The luck trade asks an Opposite to spare luck tonight, repaid tomorrow in equal portion."
"The luck trade's Artonan form ends 'em utch ya-cku durz-u hu-morit' with a curving hand sign."
"Another chain offers 'the gift of our peace of mind' as a portion of sacrifice."
"Wordchains are inherently balanced; a big luck win may be repaid by a fatal crash weeks later."
"Artonans are funny about sharing the higher-level wordchains."
"Wordchains are selected from the System and, unlike skills, can be shared with others."
"Using wordchains well requires study of the Artonan language."
"Every chain has a sacrifice half and a recipient half that balances the ledger."
"Healing chains can be said on behalf of others."
"Wordchain proficiency earns Artonan privileges, such as serving as authorized witness."
"The requester's half brings the perk; the sacrifice half repays it soon after, with a delay."
"An unpaid chain snaps back on its own terms."
"Wordchains are an ancient, simple exchange, closer to contracts than to modern spellcraft."
"Exact pronunciation, hand signs and breath pauses matter; too much wrist motion makes a dud."
"Wordchains are best learned from a private tutor."
"Velras always get a little more than they give with wordchains, a perk of their class."
"Heavy wordchain debt can drive a person mad."
"A banked positive half snaps back on its own if not spent in time."
"Almost-right chains work about ten percent of the time; practice makes chains more forgiving."
"Stronger, esoteric chains are picky, needing precise words and signs, even body modifications."
"Some chains may be taught only with formal vouching from a sponsor, such as an Artonan employer."
"Certain wizards can forcibly complete another person's chain before its time."
"Another person can call a saved half's debt due, making the chain snap back."
"Celena North counts a mastered wordchain as a talent; bestowed chains need pre-approval."
"Successful exchanges solidify a chain until even children or fingerless species can use it."
"New, forgotten, unused or one-sided chains weaken or break; many ancient chains are now broken."
"Healthy chains land their debt automatically; rarer ones come due sooner, fragile ones at once."
"Non-Avowed humans can perform many wordchains; only Avowed can do magic beyond them."
"Casting wordchains in public is a gray area, and teaching others needs permission."
"Stacked chains grow harder to call; a second chain of the same kind may fail."
"Tips cannot teach a new chain from scratch, but can improve one that works only sometimes."
"A learned chain must be cast about weekly or its gestures are forgotten."
